  "dom_id": "questionExplanation-181373",
  "name": null,
  "problem": "",
  "worked_solution": "First, we clear the rational expression by multiplying both sides by $4:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2-3p}{4} & <2p+1 \\\\ 4\\cdot (\\frac{2-3p}{4}) & <4\\cdot (2p+1) \\\\ \\cancel{4}\\cdot (\\frac{2-3p}{\\cancel{4}}) & <4\\cdot (2p+1) \\\\ 2-3p & <8p+4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we apply the addition and multiplication principles. \n\nRemember, when we divide an inequality by a negative number, we need to flip the inequality sign:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2-3p & <8p+4 \\\\ 2-3p-8p & <8p+4-8p \\\\ 2-11p & <4 \\\\ 2-11p-2 & <4-2 \\\\ -11p & <2 \\\\ \\frac{-11p}{-11} & \\,\\,>\\,\\,\\frac{2}{-11} \\\\ p & >-\\frac{2}{11}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $p>-\\frac{2}{11}.$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
  "fields": [],
  "problem_field_keys": [],
  "answer_fields_complete": null,
  "result": null,
  "proof_feedback": null,
